        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <div class="article-faq">
          <details open><summary>How does Uber use business analytics?</summary><p class="faq-a">Uber uses analytics to forecast rider demand and driver supply, set dynamic prices during busy periods, predict arrival times, and plan for locations such as airports. Its engineering team has published details on several of these systems.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How does Uber's dynamic pricing work?</summary><p class="faq-a">Uber says fares reflect the time and distance of a trip, traffic, and current rider-to-driver demand. Surge pricing is meant to attract more drivers during busy periods so riders can get a ride quickly. Uber has not published the exact formula, thresholds, or weights.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What is DeepETA?</summary><p class="faq-a">DeepETA is Uber's model for improving arrival-time estimates. It predicts the gap between its routing engine's estimate and real-world outcomes, and the resulting ETAs feed into fares, pickup estimates, rider-driver matching, and delivery planning.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Can a small business use the same analytics methods?</summary><p class="faq-a">Yes, in a simpler form. Forecasting demand with historical data, comparing forecasts to a simple baseline, and tracking results on a business dashboard are all achievable with tools like Power BI and a spreadsheet or warehouse. You don't need machine learning to get started.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What business forecasting tools should I start with?</summary><p class="faq-a">Start with the forecasting built into your BI platform or a well-structured spreadsheet model using last week's or last year's values as a baseline. Move to statistical or machine learning models only when the simple version stops being accurate enough to act on.</p></details>
